Title: The Tell-Tale Heart

Credit: Written by

Author: Edgar Allan Poe

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. DARK ROOM - NIGHT

A dimly lit room. THE NARRATOR, a man with wild eyes and a nervous demeanor, stands alone, speaking directly to us.

NARRATOR

True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR stands at the door, peering inside. The OLD MAN lies asleep in bed. The room is dark, save for a sliver of moonlight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR gently opens the door, inch by inch, holding a dark lantern. He moves with extreme caution.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work! Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR's head is inside the room. He carefully opens the lantern, casting a thin ray of light onto the OLD MAN's closed eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

And then, when my head was well in the room, I undid the lantern cautiously—oh, so cautiously—until a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR stands still, watching the OLD MAN. The eye remains closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

For seven nights I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

On the eighth night, the NARRATOR is more cautious than ever. He opens the door slowly, savoring his triumph.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR's thumb slips on the lantern's fastening. The OLD MAN stirs and sits up in bed, alarmed.

OLD MAN

Who’s there?

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR remains motionless, holding his breath. The OLD MAN listens intently, fear growing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle. Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR opens the lantern slightly, casting a ray on the OLD MAN's open eye. Fury fills the NARRATOR.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR hears the old man's heart beating loudly, growing louder and louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. It was the beating of the old man’s heart.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR, driven by the sound, leaps into the room, throwing open the lantern.

NARRATOR

With a loud yell, I threw open the lantern and leaped into the room.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The OLD MAN shrieks once. The NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the bed over him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

He shrieked once—once only. In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR examines the corpse, satisfied.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The old man was dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR dismembers the body and hides it beneath the floorboards.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I dismembered the corpse. I took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR finishes his work as the clock strikes four. A knock is heard at the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o’clock—still dark as midnight. There came a knocking at the street door.

INT. HOUSE - NIGHT

The NARRATOR opens the door to three POLICE OFFICERS. He greets them with a smile.

NARRATOR

The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Please, search the house.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR leads the OFFICERS into the room, inviting them to sit. He places his chair over the hidden body.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The OFFICERS chat casually. The NARRATOR grows pale, hearing a ringing in his ears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR becomes agitated, pacing the floor as the sound grows louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The sound increased—and what could I do? It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The NARRATOR, in a frenzy, swings his chair and screams at the OFFICERS.

NARRATOR

Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

FADE OUT.

THE END.
